<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We wish to prove that the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC _{p/} \times _{\calC } \{ x\}  \rightarrow \calC _{pv/} \times _{\calC } \{ x\}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes. Lemma <a href="/tag/5B5547" data-tag="5B5547">4.1.1.5</a> implies that the left hand side is homotopy equivalent $\bHom _{\calC }(K, \calC _{/x})$. Similarly, the right hand side can be identified with $\bHom _{\calC }(K', \calC _{/x})$. Using the functoriality implicit in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/5B5547" data-tag="5B5547">4.1.1.5</a> (see Remark <a href="/tag/E1D809" data-tag="E1D809">4.1.1.6</a>), it suffices to show that the restriction map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{\calC }(K, \calC _{/x}) \rightarrow \bHom _{\calC }(K', \calC _{/x}) \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a homotopy equivalence. Since $v$ is cofinal, this follows immediately from the fact that the projection $\calC _{/x} \rightarrow \calC $ is a right fibration. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>